C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Network Shipping Problem - Automobiles
       
      An automobile company must decide how to move cars from suppliers in Kansas City and Dallas to dealerships in New York, Minneapolis, Seattle, and San Francisco.
       
      THE PROBLEM:
       
      Each supplier has a specified number of cars in stock and each dealership has an expected number of sales. Data are as fol-lows:
       
       
      Inputs:    Suppliers   Dealers
      Cost of each route: Rows are suppliers, Coloumns are dealers.
                      New York            Minneapolis     Seattle     San Francisco
      Kansas          $4                  $12             $18         $18
      Dallas          $9                  $15             $17         $21              
       
      Determine the shipping volumes that will minimze cost.
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Minimize shipping cost.
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 15, 2015
  26   
  27  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  28  SETS suppliers  cars in stock /Kansas, Dallas/
  29       dealers expected sales /Newyork, Minneapolis, Seattle, Sanfrancisco/;
  30   
  31  * 2. DEFINE input data
  32  PARAMETERS
  33     c(suppliers) Capacity of cars
  34           /Kansas 1000,
  35            Dallas 800 /
  36   
  37     b(dealers) Expected sales in quantity of cars
  38            /Newyork 400,
  39             Minneapolis  250,
  40             Seattle  450,
  41             Sanfrancisco 450/;
  42   
  43  TABLE A(suppliers,dealers) Cost of each supplier to dealer route
  44                   Newyork  Minneapolis  Seattle  Sanfrancisco
  45   Kansas          4        12           18       18
  46   Dallas          9        15           17       21;
  47   
  48   
  49  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  50  VARIABLES
  51      X(suppliers,dealers) Quantity of cars to send from one supplier to one dealer 8 dimensions total,
  52      VCOST  total cost ($);
  53  *X1 From Kansas to Newyork
  54  *X2 From Kansas to Minneapolis
  55  *X3 From kansas to Seattle
  56  *X4 From Kansas to San Francisco
  57  *Y1 From Dallas to Newyork
  58  *Y2 From Dallas to Minneapolis
  59  *Y3 From Dallas to Seattle
  60  *Y4 From Dallas to San Francicso
  61   
  62   
  63  * Non-negativity constraints
  64  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  65   
  66  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  67  EQUATIONS
  68     COST Total cost ($) and objective function value
  69     RES_CONSTRAIN1(suppliers) constraint of how many cars are in stock for each supplier,RES_CONSTRAIN2(dealers) constraint of minimum number of cars sold by each dealer ;
  70   
  71  COST..                 VCOST =E= SUM((suppliers,dealers), A(suppliers,dealers)*X(suppliers,dealers));
  72  RES_CONSTRAIN1(suppliers) ..    SUM(dealers, X(suppliers,dealers)) =L= c(suppliers);
  73  RES_CONSTRAIN2(dealers) ..    SUM(suppliers, X(suppliers,dealers)) =G= b(dealers);
  74   
  75   
  76  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  77  MODEL NETWORK /COST, RES_CONSTRAIN1,RES_CONSTRAIN2/;
  78  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  79  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  80   
  81  OPTION LIMROW = 10;
  82  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  83  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  84  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  85  SOLVE NETWORK USING LP MINIMIZING VCOST;
  86   
  87   
  88  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


